    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for this. i think we'll focus heavily on the price stability and the economic adjustment clauses. one thing i'm stuck on is how to phrase the \"force majeure\" section. we want it to cover major disruptions, but we also want to make sure the supplier doesn't use every little hiccup as an excuse to skip a delivery or hike the price. how do i define the line between a legitimate excuse and just \"bad faith\" attempts to avoid their obligations?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To define the line between a legitimate excuse and \"bad faith\" attempts, consider a \"common sense test\" clause in your force majeure section. This test assesses the gravity and unexpectedness of the disruption, rather than the contradictory intentions of the supplier.\n\nFor example, you could include a provision stating that the supplier's obligation to deliver materials is suspended only if the disruption meets the following criteria: \n\n1. It is governed by law or beyond the supplier's control (such as natural disasters, wars, etc.)\n2. It is of a gross and unforeseeable nature ( urgently suspected, expected, or somewhat anticipated)\n3. The supplier with reasonable conviction produced evidence that the disruption harms the supplier's ultimate ability to supply\n\nThis approach allows for flexibility and encourages suppliers to be objective in determining the legitimacy of a force majeure event.",
      "source": "target",
